course: dictionary
lesson: 6041
title: Finding meaning in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A membrane is a very thin layer, like a skin, that covers or lines something. In «Animal cells, like the soft roundish cells scraped from your cheek, have a thin skin called a membrane instead of a stiff wall», the words thin skin and called tell you that a membrane is the name for that thin skin.
explain: The words instead of a stiff wall are a clue too. Animal cells have no hard wall, so the membrane is soft and thin, not hard. Ask yourself what would fit in that place, then try your meaning in the sentence: a thin layer called a membrane instead of a stiff wall still makes sense.
fact: A membrane is a very thin layer, like a skin.
fact: The cell membrane is the thin outer layer around every living cell, and it controls what goes in and out.
fact: The cell membrane lets water, food and oxygen in, and waste out.
fact: The eardrum is a membrane that vibrates with sound.
fact: Builders use waterproof membranes under floors and roofs.
exercise: «Animal cells... have a thin skin called a membrane instead of a stiff wall.» What is a membrane? | A hard, stiff wall | *A thin layer, like a skin | A kind of bone | A very large cell
exercise: The cell membrane controls what goes in and out of a cell. What does it control? | *What goes in and out | The number of cells in the body | The colour of the cell
exercise: Builders put a waterproof membrane under a roof. What is its job? | *To keep water out | To make the roof bend | To make the roof glow
test: Which part of the ear is a membrane that vibrates with sound? | Your eyelid | *The eardrum | Your nose
test: The cell membrane lets oxygen in. What does it let out? | Oxygen | Sound | Food | *Waste
exam: Which word means a very thin layer, like a skin, that covers or lines part of a living thing? | Wall | *Membrane | Cell | Eardrum
